{"task_id": "HumanEval/54", "solution": "def same_chars(s0: str, s1: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if two words have the same characters.\n    >>> same_chars('eabcdzzzz', 'dddzzzzzzzddeddabc')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('abcd', 'dddddddabc')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('dddddddabc', 'abcd')\n    True\n    >>> same_chars('eabcd', 'dddddddabc')\n    False\n    >>> same_chars('abcd', 'dddddddabce')\n    False\n    >>> same_chars('eabcdzzzz', 'dddzzzzzzzddddabc')\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    import unicodedata\n\n    # As per design context, normalization includes stripping whitespace,\n    # applying Unicode NFC, and is case-sensitive by default.\n    UNICODE_FORM = \"NFC\"\n    CASE_SENSITIVE = True\n\n    def _normalize_string(text: str) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Applies whitespace stripping and Unicode normalization.\"\"\"\n        s = text.strip()\n        s = unicodedata.normalize(UNICODE_FORM, s)\n        # The design states comparisons are case-sensitive by default, so casefolding is skipped here.\n        return s\n\n    # Normalize both input strings according to the defined rules.\n    normalized_s0 = _normalize_string(s0)\n    normalized_s1 = _normalize_string(s1)\n\n    # Create a set of unique characters for each normalized string.\n    # The comparison is set-based, meaning character counts do not matter.\n    set_s0 = set(normalized_s0)\n    set_s1 = set(normalized_s1)\n\n    # Return True if both sets of characters are identical.\n    return set_s0 == set_s1"}
